      "id": 1,
      "claim": "The belief that flies arise from rotting meat is the doctrine of spontaneous generation.",
      "evidence": "Historical texts describe spontaneous generation as life originating from non‑living matter; e.g., Aristotle and pre‑19th‑century naturalists.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Francesco Redi (1668) showed that flies only appear on meat when it is exposed to air, using covered and uncovered jars.",
      "evidence": "Redi’s experiment demonstrated that maggots come from eggs laid by adult flies, not from the meat itself.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Louis Pasteur (1859) used swan‑neck flasks to prove that broth remains sterile unless exposed to airborne microorganisms.",
      "evidence": "Pasteur’s results showed no microbial growth in sealed flasks, disproving that microorganisms arise spontaneously in nutrient broth.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Both Redi’s and Pasteur’s work relied on repeated, controlled experiments rather than a single new instrument or discovery.",
      "evidence": "Redi’s experiment required no microscope; Pasteur’s conclusion depended on the experimental design (sealed vs open) and was reproduced many times, illustrating the role of continued experimentation.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore the primary reason the idea was disproved is continued experimentation.",
      "evidence": "Option (C) directly matches the methodological cause; options (A) atom discovery, (B) surgical techniques, and (D) microscope invention are unrelated to the key experimental refutation.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "confidence": 0.97,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "A",
      "reason_rejected": "The discovery of the atom concerns physics and does not address biological generation of flies."
    },
    {
      "answer": "B",
      "reason_rejected": "Better surgical techniques are unrelated to the theory of spontaneous generation."
    },
    {
      "answer": "D",
      "reason_rejected": "Microscopes aided observation of microbes but the decisive disproof of flies from meat came from controlled experiments, not the instrument itself."
    }
  ]
